> I don't have one of these, so correct me if I'm wrong...  couldn't you
> take a USB flash drive, create 2 or 3 partitions, an ext2/3 for linux,
> an NTFS or FAT16/32 for windows, and/or HFS/HFS+ for Mac (omit any as
> necessary), install a copy of nethack on each partition, then plug
> into any USB compatible computer. 

Actually, you don't need to have different filesystems. At least, Mac
OS-X recognizes the VFS filesystem that seems to be standard for the
flash drives. I haven't tried it under Linux (yet), but I'd be amazed
if any recent distro was missing the drivers for that.

I'm not sure if the Unix/OS-X pre-compiled binaries expect data files
to be in /usr/games or if they are more flexible than they used to
be. The Win32 version seems to expect the playground to be in the same
directory as the .exe file, which makes it superbly portable.

> A 128 MB USB flash drive won't cost more than $30-40, depending on
